{
  "dom_id": "step-q113672",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{4/5}.$",
  "worked_solution": "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{4}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{4/5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{4/5+1}}{(\\frac{4}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/5}}{(\\frac{9}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{9}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{9/5})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{9}\\cdot \\frac{9}{5}{x}^{9/5-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{4/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
